@h1 Reusable pieces h1@

Wit lets you define your own nodes and use them anywhere. Name a piece of
writing once, then drop it into your prose by name — and if you change the
definition, every use updates. This is how a manuscript stays consistent: one
source of truth for a recurring value, a chapter template, a citation format, a
callout.

@h2 Define once, use everywhere h2@

A definition begins with a hash and a name; a use is the same name with an
at-sign. Hash defines, at-sign uses. That is the whole relationship.

@h2 A single-line value h2@

The simplest definition names a short value. A colon after the name means "no
body":

@@@pre
#year: 1923

The lamp was first lit in @year, and relit every winter since.
pre@@@

The reference stands in for the value, so you write the year once and mention it
by name as often as you like. Change the definition and every mention follows.

@h2 A value block h2@

When the value is longer — a few lines, its own paragraph, some emphasis — wrap
it between a colon and a closing double-bang fence:

@@@pre
#epigraph:
The sea does not forgive forgetting.
— coastal proverb
!!

@epigraph
pre@@@

Now the epigraph is defined in one place and rendered wherever you write its
name. This is the preferred way to name any chunk of content.

@h2 A block definition with a body slot h2@

The most powerful form is a wrapper: a definition whose body has a slot where the
_caller's_ content will render. You write the surrounding structure once, mark
the slot with three dots, and every use fills it:

@@@pre
#chapter
@hr hr@

...
chapter#

@chapter
The road climbed for an hour before the house appeared.
chapter@
pre@@@

The three dots are where the invocation's body drops in. Notice the two fences:
a block definition opens with just the hash-name (no colon) and closes with the
name and a hash.

@h2 Captures — parameters for your own nodes h2@

A definition can name parameters and interpolate them into its body. You declare
the parameter names between double bars, and drop each one in with a
double-colon wrapper:

@@@pre
#chapter ||number, title||
@h2 Chapter ::number:: — ::title:: h2@

...
chapter#

@chapter |number I| |title The Arrival|
The road climbed for an hour before the house appeared.
chapter@
pre@@@

The call supplies "I" and "The Arrival"; the definition places them in the
heading and renders the body where the dots are. One template, consistent
chapters.

@aside(class note) 💡 A double-colon capture such as @@@code ::title:: code@@@
only lives _inside_ a definition body. It is not a way to interpolate a value
into ordinary prose — for that, define a value and reference it by name. aside@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li _A comma inside a record field value._ Commas separate fields, so a comma in
a value breaks the record. Keep values comma-free, or use a table's cell wrapper
for tabular data. See @a |href /docs/write/tables/| Tables a@. li@
@li _Expecting a capture to work in prose._ A double-colon capture only resolves
inside a definition body, never in running text. li@
@li _A hash glued to a letter at the top of a file._ A line that opens with a
hash pressed against a word is read as a definition and can silently swallow the
line. Keep a hash off a following letter in prose — see
@a |href /docs/write/prose/| Paragraphs & prose a@. li@
ul@

